## Reflection: Design Thinking (Low-Fidelity Prototype)

### What I Learned
Through the Design Thinking project, our group proposed the UTM Smart Parking Availability
and Reservation Platform, which focuses on improving campus mobility and user experience.
This project helped me understand how design thinking can be used to solve real-world
problems by focusing on users’ needs. By following the empathy, define, ideate, prototype,
and test phases, I learned how to analyze problems systematically and design practical,
user-centered solutions.

### Suggested Improvements
One improvement for this project would be to involve more users during the testing phase.
Collecting feedback from a wider range of campus users could help improve the usability
and effectiveness of the proposed interface.

### Personal Reflection
Overall, this project enhanced my creativity, teamwork, and problem-solving skills.
It showed me how technology and design can work together to support decision-making and
improve daily experiences on campus. This activity also increased my interest in developing
digital solutions for real organizational challenges.
